n-3,3) instead? At x = n-3, y = 0, not 3. So no.\n\nCould L3 (currently covering D2 = (n-2,3) and (n-3,2)) also include (n-3,1) or (n-3,3)? Let's check line through (n-2,3) and (n-3,2) has slope 1, equation y = x - n + 5. At x = n-3, y = (n-3) - n + 5 = 2, which is (n-3,2). So cannot include (n-3,1) or (n-3,3). Could we change L3 to cover (n-3,3) instead of (n-3,2)? Let's try line through (n-2,3) and (n-3,3). Slope = (3-3)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 0/(-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So no.\n\nCould we change L3 to cover (n-3,1) instead? Slope = (1-3)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = -2/(-1) = 2, allowed. So line through (n-2,3) and (n-3,1) slope 2. Equation: y - 3 = 2 (x - (n-2)) => y = 2x - 2n + 7. At x = n-3, y = 2(n-3) - 2n + 7 = 2n - 6 - 2n + 7 = 1, matches (n-3,1). At x = n-1, y = 2(n-1) - 2n + 7 = 2n - 2 - 2n + 7 = 5, not in column n-1 (max y=2). So L3 would cover D2 and (n-3,1). That's okay.\n\nNow we have L3 covering D2 and (n-3,1). L4 covering D1 and (n-1,1). L2 covering D3 and (n-2,1). L1 covering D4 and (n-2,2). We still have lower point (n-3,2) and (n-3,3) unassigned. Actually we used (n-3,2) earlier? No, we haven't assigned (n-3,2) or (n-3,3). Let's check: L3 now covers (n-3,1). L4 covers (n-1,1). L2 covers (n-2,1). L1 covers (n-2,2). So we still need to cover (n-3,2) and (n-3,3). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust again.\n\nPerhaps we can assign (n-3,2) to L2 or L1. Let's see.\n\nL2 currently covers D3 = (n-1,2) and (n-2,1). Could we also include (n-3,2)? Check collinearity: line through (n-1,2) and (n-2,1) has equation y = x - n + 3. At x = n-3, y = (n-3) - n + 3 = 0, not 2. So no.\n\nL1 currently covers D4 = (n,1) and (n-2,2). Could we also include (n-3,2)? Check line through (n,1) and (n-2,2) slope = (2-1)/(n-2 - n) = 1/(-2) = -0.5. Equation: y - 1 = -0.5 (x - n). At x = n-3, y = 1 - 0.5*(n-3 - n) = 1 - 0.5*(-3) = 1 + 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So cannot.\n\nCould we adjust L1 to cover (n-3,2) instead of (n-2,2)? Let's try line through (n,1) and (n-3,2). Slope = (2-1)