ical line. To cover them with a non-vertical line, we need a line that passes through at least two points in that column, but they have same x coordinate, so any line passing through two points with same x coordinate must be vertical. So a sunny line cannot cover more than one point in a given column (since any line passing through two points with same x coordinate is vertical). So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. Similarly, each sunny line can cover at most one point per row (since horizontal lines are non-sunny). And each sunny line can cover at most one point per diagonal (x+y constant) because slope -1 lines are non-sunny. So a sunny line can intersect each column at most once, each row at most once, each diagonal at most once. So a sunny line can cover at most one point from each column, row, diagonal. But there are many columns (n columns). So a sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). But the total number of points is n(n+1)/2, which is larger than n for n≥3. So we need multiple lines.\n\nBut the constraints might limit the number of sunny lines because each column has many points, and we need to cover them with vertical lines (non-sunny) or sunny lines. If we have many sunny lines, we might need to use vertical lines for the rest. But we can have many sunny lines as long as we have enough vertical lines to cover the remaining points. The total number of lines is n. So if we have k sunny lines, we have n-k non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. But we can choose vertical lines for simplicity. Each vertical line x=c covers n+1-c points. So the total number of points covered by vertical lines is sum_{c in S} (n+1-c) where S is the set of columns we choose to have vertical lines. The remaining points must be covered by sunny lines. So we need to see if for a given k we can choose a set of n-k columns to be vertical lines such that the remaining points can be covered by k sunny lines.\n\nWe