use that. Use line through (1,1)-(1,2) vertical not sunny. Use line through (1,2)-(2,1) slope -1 not sunny. So any line covering two of the points is non-sunny. So each sunny line can cover at most one point. So we need at least 3 sunny lines to cover 3 points. That's okay. So we can have k=3 for n=3. The solution includes k=3. So that's fine.\n\nNow for n=4, we found a configuration with k=4 (all sunny) covering all points using 3 sunny lines covering all points and a fourth redundant sunny line. So k=4 possible for n=4. Let's verify that the 3 sunny lines we used indeed cover all points. We used:\n\nLine L1: y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2))\nLine L2: line through (1,2) and (3,1) slope -1/2 (covers (1,2),(3,1))\nLine L3: line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope -2 (covers (1,3),(2,1))\n\nWe also need to cover (1,4) and (2,3) and (3,2) and (4,1)? Wait for n=4, points are (a,b) with a+b ≤ 5. Let's list all points:\n\na=1: b=1,2,3,4 (since 1+4=5)\na=2: b=1,2,3 (2+3=5)\na=3: b=1,2 (3+2=5)\na=4: b=1 (4+1=5)\n\nSo points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 10 points.\n\nOur lines covered (1,1),(2,2) by L1; (1,2),(3,1) by L2; (1,3),(2,1) by L3. That's 6 points covered. We still need to cover (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). We haven't covered those. So our earlier covering was incomplete. We need to cover all 10 points. Let's see if we can cover them with 3 sunny lines plus a redundant line. Let's try to find 4 sunny lines that cover all points. Perhaps we can use lines that pass through more points.\n\nWe need to find a set of 4 sunny lines that cover all points. Let's try to find a systematic approach.\n\nWe can try to use lines with slope 1 (y=x) covering (1,1),(2,2),(3,3?) but (3,3) not in set because 3+3=6 >5. So only (1,1),(2,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine with slope 2: y=2x passes through (1,2),(2,4) but (2,4) not in set because 2+4=6 >5. So only (1,2). So not helpful.\n\nLine with slope 1/2: y = (1/2)x + b. Let's try to find lines that pass through multiple points. For points (a,b) and (c,d) to be collinear with slope m = (d-b)/(c-a). For them to be sunny, m must not be 0, infinite, -1. So many slopes possible.\n\nWe need to cover points in a triangular shape. Perhaps we can use lines that go through points across different rows and columns.\n\nLet's try to find a covering with 4 sunny lines. Since we have 10 points, each line can cover at most maybe 3 points? Let's see if we can find lines covering 3 points each. For example, line through (1,1),(2,3),(3,5?) but (3,5) not in set. So not.\n\nLine through (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) is slope -1, which is not sunny. So can't use that.\n\nLine through (1,4),(2,2) slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. That line also passes through (3,0) not in set. So covers (1,4),(2,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine through (1,4),(3,2) slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (1,4),(4,1) slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,3),(3,2) slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (2,3),(4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n\nLine through (3,2),(4,1) slope (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, not sunny.\n\nThus many pairs have slope -1, which is not allowed. So we need to avoid those.\n\nLet's try to find a covering using vertical lines for some columns. But vertical lines are non-sunny. So we need to allocate some non-sunny lines. Since we want k=4 sunny lines, we have n=4 lines total, so we have 0 non-sunny lines. So all lines must be sunny. So we cannot use vertical lines. So we need to cover all points with 4 sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's try to see if we can cover all 10 points with 4 sunny lines. Each line can intersect each column at most once. There are 4 columns. So each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So 4 lines can cover at most 16 points, which is enough. But we need to ensure that each column's points are covered. Column 1 has 4 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Since each sunny line can intersect column 1 at most once, we need at least 4 sunny lines to cover all points in column 1. So we need exactly 4 sunny lines, each covering a distinct point in column 1. So each line must pass through a distinct point in column 1. So we can assign each line to cover one of those points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then each line also can intersect other columns at some points. So we need to see if we can assign lines such that they also cover all points in other columns.\n\nLet's label the lines L_a for a=1..4, each passes through (1,a). Then we need to cover points in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Each line can intersect column 2 at most once. So we need to assign each of the three points to some of the four lines, possibly leaving one line not intersecting column 2. Similarly for column 3: (3,1),(3,2). And column 4: (4,1). So we need to find lines through (1,a) that also pass through appropriate points in other columns.\n\nWe can treat this as a matching problem: we need to assign each point in column j to a line that passes through (1,a) and (j,b). The line determined by two points (1,a) and (j,b) has slope (b-a)/(j-1). This slope must not be 0, infinite, -1. Since j>1, slope is defined. We need to ensure that for each line, the slope is not -1, 0, infinite. Since (1,a) and (j,b) have different x coordinates, slope is finite. It could be 0 if b=a, i.e., horizontal line. So we must avoid b=a. Also slope -1 if (b-a) = -(j-1) => b = a - (j-1). Since b must be positive integer ≤ n+1-j, we need to avoid that.\n\nWe can try to assign points to lines. Let's try to assign:\n\nLine L1 passes through (1,1). It can also pass through (2,2) (b=2). Slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, which is sunny. So L1 can cover (2,2). It can also pass through (3,3) but (3,3) not in set. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\nLine L2 passes through (1,2). It can also pass through (2,1) (b=1). Slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So can't use (2,1). It can pass through (2,3) (b=3). Slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. So L2 can cover (2,3). It can also pass through (3,2) (b=2). Slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed. So can't use (3,2). It can pass through (3,4) not in set. So L2 covers (1,2) and (2,3).\n\nLine L3 passes through (1,3). It can pass through (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So L3 can cover (2,1). It can also pass through (2,2) slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. It can pass through (2,3) slope = (3-3)/(2-1) = 0, not allowed. So L3 covers (2,1). It can also pass through (3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, sunny. So L3 can also cover (3,2). So L3 covers (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). That's good.\n\nLine L4 passes through (1,4). It can pass through (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So L4 can cover (2,2). It can also pass through (3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2, sunny. So L4 can cover (3,1). It can also pass through (4,1) slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So can't cover (4,1). But we still need to cover (4,1). Maybe L1 can cover (4,1)? Let's see: L1 passes through (1,1) and (2,2). The line equation is y = x. Does it pass through (4,1)? No. L2 passes through (1,2) and (2,3) => line y = x+1. Does it pass through (4,1)? No. L3 passes through (1,3) and (2,1) => slope -2, line equation y = -2(x-1)+3 = -2x+5. At x=4, y = -8+5 = -3, not 1. L4 passes through (1,4) and (2,2) => slope -2, line equation y = -2(x-1)+4 = -2x+6. At x=4, y = -8+6 = -2, not 1. So none of the lines cover (4,1). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe could try a different assignment: maybe L1 covers (1,1) and (4,1) using slope 0 (horizontal) which is not allowed. So can't.\n\nThus covering all points with 4 sunny lines might be impossible for n=4. Let's try to see if any covering exists. Since column 1 has 4 points, we need 4 lines each covering one of them. So each line is determined by its point in column 1 and some other point(s). We need to cover all points in other columns. Let's try to systematically search for a covering. But we can reason: column 4 has only point (4,1). That point must be covered by one of the 4 lines. That