 through (2,2) (slope 1), (3,3) not in set, (4,4) not in set. So L1 can cover (2,2). It can also pass through (3,1) slope = (1-1)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed. So can't cover (3,1). It can pass through (2,3) slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, sunny, but (2,3) is in column 2. So L1 can also cover (2,3). However, line through (1,1) and (2,3) has slope 2, equation y = 2x -1. At x=3, y = 5, not in set. So L1 can cover (1,1) and (2,3). It can also cover (2,2) if we choose that instead. But we need to decide.\n\nLine through (1,3) can also pass through (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So L2 can cover (2,1). It can also pass through (3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, sunny. So L2 can cover (3,2). It can also pass through (2,2) slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So L2 cannot cover (2,2). So L2 can cover (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nLine through (1,4) can also pass through (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So L3 can cover (2,2). It can also pass through (3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2, sunny. So L3 can cover (3,1). It can also pass through (2,3) slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So L3 can cover (2,2) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover all points: Let's assign:\n\n- L4: (1,2)-(4,1) covers (1,2),(4,1).\n- L1: (1,1)-(2,3) covers (1,1),(2,3).\n- L2: (1,3)-(2,1)-(3,2) covers (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). Actually line through (1,3) and (2,1) also passes through (3,-1) not in set, but we can also consider line through (1,3) and (3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, which passes through (2,2.5) not integer. So we cannot have both (2,1) and (3,2) on same line with (1,3). Let's check: line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope = -2, equation y = -2(x-1)+3 = -2x+5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1, not (3,2). So line through (1,3) and (2,1) does not pass through (3,2). So we need to decide which points L2 covers. It can cover either (2,1) or (3,2) but not both. Let's see if we can cover the other point with L3.\n\n- L3: (1,4)-(2,2)-(3,1) covers (1,4),(2,2),(3,1). Indeed line through (1,4) and (2,2) slope = -2, 